  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   I propose a multi-dimensional search strategy that explicitly targets length optimization while combining elements of the parent's heuristic initializations, frequency-domain analysis, and gradient-based fine-tuning that proved effective.\n   Rationale:\n   - The step function's autocorrelation properties depend critically on sequence length\n   - Length variation introduces fundamentally different solution topologies\n   - Long sequences may produce smoother autocorrelations with lower peak concentrations\n   - However, the score function (2n * max_conv) grows with length, creating a tradeoff\n   - The previous approach using 1600 performed well, but systematic length exploration may yield better candidates\n   - We should individually optimize sequences across several promising lengths (1000, 1600, 2000) and return the best\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   We will consider candidate sequences of three lengths: 1000, 1600 (parent), and 2000.\n   For each length n:\n     - Initialize with a sequence that maintains two orthogonal components from parent\n     - Apply frequency-domain shaping via inverse FFT to minimize sharp peaks\n     - Use active-set constraint solving to enforce L_infinity bounds on autocorrelation\n   Optimization variables:\n     - Non-negative coefficients (length n) with fixed L1 norm ceil(sqrt(2n))\n     - Subject to constraints that bound the autocorrelation\n   Formulation:\n     a = [a0, a1, ..., a_{n-1}] in R^n_{+}\n     with \u2211a_i = ceil(sqrt(2n))\n     and (a * a)[k] / (\u2211a_i)^2 <= M (with M chosen gradient-adaptively)\n   The objective is min 2n * max_k (a*a)[k] / (\u2211a_i)^2\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a. Read the parent sequence (height_sequence_1) if available\n   b. For each candidate length (start with parent length):\n        i. If parent available: initialize with stretched/interpolated parent\n           Else: use uniform baseline\n        ii. Reshape to candidate length (interpolate or embed)\n        iii. Normalize L1 norm to ceil(sqrt(2 * candidate_length))\n        iv. Design frequency-domain spectrum to avoid sharp peaks (avoid low frequencies)\n        v. Initialize an empty active-set for candidate length\n        vi. Run a time-limited LP best-response phase (enhanced version)\n   c. After core optimization:\n        i. Verify non-negativity and finite score\n        ii. Compare across candidate lengths and dimensions using exact evaluator\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   a. Main candidate lengths: 1000, 1600, 2000\n   b. Use parent's architecture at adjacent lengths\n   c. Apply two initialization methods per length:\n        - Uniform distribution\n        - Symmetric sinusoidal embedding\n   d. Time budget per candidate: proportional to sequence length (1600: 800s, others: ceil)\n   e. Variation: frequency-domain polynomial resampling between lags\n   f. Variation: explicit damping of extreme lags\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   a. Initial: 0.3s per candidate for validation and resampling\n   b. Core LP optimization: 1500s available\n   c. Allocate in steps:\n        i. 75s for LP+subgradient of each candidate length\n        ii. 300s for coordinate descent polishing\n        iii. 1500s for active-set tailored optimization (but limited)\n   d. Robustness measures:\n        i. Ensure L1 normalization after interpolation\n        ii. Clip coefficients between (0, 1000)\n        iii. Maximum length: 2000 (to finish in 600s total)\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   The final sequence must be:\n     1. Non-negative float list\n     2. Sum >= 0.01\n     3. Each element <= 1000.0\n   We compare using evaluate_sequence:\n     - Must be called with input sequence\n     - Must be called with output sequence\n   The algorithm:\n     ```python\n     def propose_candidate():\n         candidates = [(1000, 'uniform'), (1600, None), (2000, 'uniform')]\n         scores = [np.inf]*len(candidates)\n         seqs = [None]*len(candidates)\n         t_total = 1800.0\n         t_seq_budget = max(1.0, t_total/4.0) # in seconds\n         ...\n     ```",
